Domain 3, Security Architecture 3.1 #1 — Cloud Infrastructures

The big picture for Domain 3: Domain 2 was about what attacks look like. Domain 3 is about how you build things so they’re harder to attack. 
What are the different ways to set up IT infrastructure, and what security trade-offs does each one bring?

This is like a set of choices about where you live:

Shared responsibility model. Renting vs owning. If you rent (cloud), the landlord fixes the roof and the plumbing, but locking your door and protecting your valuables
is still your job. The split changes with the cloud type. With IaaS you’re renting an empty house, so you handle more yourself. With SaaS it’s more like a serviced
apartment, where the provider does most of the work and you just look after your own account and data. The key exam point is that your data is always your responsibility.
Hybrid cloud. Living partly in your own house and partly in a rental. Now you have two sets of locks, two security setups, and the path between them to protect.
More moving parts means more chances for a gap. Third-party vendors. Tradespeople you let into the house. You need to trust them, check them, and control what they
can reach. ( back to the supply-chain attacks in 2.3.) Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Instead of building each house by hand, you use one master blueprint.
Every house comes out the same, so there’s no “oh, we forgot the lock on that one.” Consistency is the security win here. Serverless. You don’t own or rent a house at all. 
You just book a room for the exact minutes you need it. The provider handles everything underneath, and you only look after your bit of code.
Monolithic vs microservices. A monolith is one giant building: if one part breaks or gets compromised, the whole thing can be affected.
Microservices are a row of small separate units, each doing one job. If one is broken into, the others can stay standing. You get better isolation,
but there are more doors to guard.























